=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules matching all traffic arriving on its eth2 interface.

**Mechanism:** Router6's eth2 connects to router3 via the `r6_r3` link — the sole path between the rest of the network (Locations A, C, and WAN routers) and the 172.18.51.x/mswitch2 segment behind router6. Three iptables rules (`-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`) drop every forwarded packet that arrives on that interface, covering TCP, UDP, and all other protocols. Any traffic from router3 destined for subnets behind router6 (172.18.51.x on switch10, and the switch11 segment) is silently discarded before it can be forwarded inward. Return path is equally broken: reply packets from those subnets reach router3 and come back in via eth2, where they are also dropped.

**Localization:** Router6, eth2 (interface `r6_r3`). Confirmed by infra_sweep directly extracting the three iptables FORWARD DROP rules from that device. No other device carries matching drop rules.

**Root cause:** Three blanket FORWARD DROP rules on router6's eth2 interface block all transit traffic between router3 and the mswitch2/switch10/switch11 subnets, fully isolating those subnets from the rest of the topology.

**Fix:** On router6, delete the three rules — `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` — to restore forwarding through the router3 link.
=== END ===